# lychee link-checker ignore patterns
# Matched against full URLs. See https://lychee.cli.rs/#/usage/config for syntax.
#
# Add entries when a real URL 429s or rate-limits CI-side but is actually valid.
# Do NOT add entries for genuinely broken links — fix the link instead.

# GitHub issues / PRs that may not exist at check time (404 on dynamic paths
# is acceptable when the CI is run before the referenced PR is filed)
^https://github\.com/.*/(issues|pull|discussions)/new

# Microsoft documentation frequently returns 403 to unauthenticated IPs
# from cloud CI runners. The pages are real; access is geo-gated or
# UA-gated. Safe to ignore.
^https://learn\.microsoft\.com/
^https://docs\.microsoft\.com/

# Microsoft Download Center — individual download paths are reorganized
# without redirects (e.g. the HttpPlatformHandler MSI moved silently,
# producing a 404 in run 25764379480). The host is stable but CI cannot
# reach most paths unauthenticated anyway. Same suppression pattern as
# learn.microsoft.com above.
^https://download\.microsoft\.com/

# Office 365 Management Activity API service endpoints. These are API roots,
# not web pages: the service answers under /api/v1.0/{tenant}/activity/feed/...
# and returns a permanent 404 at the host root, so lychee reads a correct,
# by-design response as a dead link. All four are cited in
# docs/customer-documents/operational-guides/siem-telemetry-emission/03-m365-unified-audit.qmd
# as the per-cloud audience values an app registration must target, and the
# host set is documented by Microsoft at
# learn.microsoft.com/office/office-365-management-api/troubleshooting-the-office-365-management-activity-api
# Ignoring the hosts rather than the bare roots so future pages may cite the
# /api/... paths, which are equally uncheckable without an auth token.
^https://manage\.office\.com(/|$)
^https://manage-gcc\.office\.com(/|$)
^https://manage\.office365\.us(/|$)
^https://manage\.protection\.apps\.mil(/|$)

# Archive.org snapshots are occasionally slow/timeouts in CI
^https://web\.archive\.org/

# FedRAMP PMO site (fedramp.gov) returns 403 to unauthenticated cloud CI
# runners — same pattern as learn.microsoft.com above. The /resources/documents/
# and /rev5/documents-templates paths were also reorganized in the 2025 FedRAMP
# 20x relaunch and no longer resolve. Provenance tables and ADRs that cite these
# URLs are historical records, not navigable references. Fix live links manually
# if fedramp.gov ever provides stable, CI-accessible URLs.
^https://www\.fedramp\.gov/

# Anchor-only links within rendered Quarto pages that lychee resolves
# against fragments ('#section'); handled by quarto render, not lychee
^#

# Diagram pipeline patterns
# Local `.html` link targets. A `.qmd` cites its sibling as `foo.html` because
# that is the path in the *rendered* site; the file only exists in `_site` after
# a Quarto render, so a source-tree check always reports it missing. Suppressing
# the rendered extension is not the same as suppressing local links: this used
# to read `^file://`, which turned off local link checking for the entire
# corpus — 4,656 links, every relative path in canon and docs, unchecked and
# silently so. Narrowed 2026-08-25; do not widen it back.
^file://.*\.html$
\.mmd$
UIAO-DIAGRAM
UIAO-INJECT

# Archived pre-split monorepo (WhalerMike/uiao-core was removed after the
# four-repo consolidation into WhalerMike/uiao). Issue/PR/discussion URLs in
# CHANGELOG.md and ADRs are historical provenance records, not navigable links.
^https://github\.com/WhalerMike/uiao-core/

# GitHub profile that no longer exists (referenced in core/CHANGELOG.md).
^https://github\.com/endgantt\b

# Cisco documentation — URL restructuring caused 404s on the SD-WAN router
# series page referenced in docs/docs/vendor_stack_v1.0.qmd.
# Cisco reorganises /c/en/us/support/ paths without redirects.
^https://www\.cisco\.com/

# Microsoft Graph API endpoints appear as URLs in contributor setup docs but
# are not navigable web pages — they return 400/401/403 from unauthenticated
# cloud CI runners. Same suppression pattern as learn.microsoft.com above.
# .us is the Microsoft Graph host for the GCC High government cloud and
# is referenced in the customer-doc Intune policy templates.
^https://graph\.microsoft\.com/
^https://graph\.microsoft\.us/

# Microsoft Entra portal returns 403 to unauthenticated cloud CI runners.
# Same suppression pattern as learn.microsoft.com above.
^https://entra\.microsoft\.com/

# Claude.ai session/workspace URLs are authenticated per-user surfaces — they
# return 302/403 to unauthenticated cloud CI runners. They appear in
# inbox/ session-export markdown as nav chrome from the Claude web UI.
^https://claude\.ai/

# GitHub repository settings pages (secrets, actions, permissions) require
# admin auth and return 404 to unauthenticated cloud CI runners. They appear
# in session-log setup instructions as operator walk-through URLs.
^https://github\.com/WhalerMike/[^/]+/settings/

# GitHub security advisory creation pages require authentication and redirect
# to login for anonymous requests, which makes them unsuitable for CI link
# checking. Treat these like other authenticated GitHub UI endpoints.
^https://github\.com/WhalerMike/[^/]+/security/advisories/new$

# Template URLs with unsubstituted shell variables. These appear as literal
# strings in contributor setup docs (e.g. contributor-tier1-setup.qmd) where
# the reader is expected to substitute their own tenant ID before using them.
# Lychee tries to fetch the raw literal and the target service returns 4xx/5xx.
\$UIAO_[A-Z_]+

# json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema returns transient 503s under CI load.
# The URL is the canonical identifier for JSON Schema 2020-12 and is embedded
# as a reference in multiple canon + modernization docs — it does not need to
# resolve at check time.
^https://json-schema\.org/draft/2020-12/schema

# Internal example hostnames embedded in customer-facing docs (Gitea
# servers, doc portals, webhook receivers, CRL distribution points). They
# appear inside code blocks alongside fictional organizations like contoso
# and are never intended to resolve from CI runners. Without these the
# customer-document corpus floods the link checker with hundreds of
# unreachable .local / .contoso.* URLs.
https?://[^/]+\.local(/|:|$)
https?://[^/]+\.contoso\.(com|local)(/|:|$)
https?://crl\.yourdomain\.com
# Generic internal-feed example host used in platform-server-build.qmd §0.5
# (Artifactory / Azure Artifacts mirror placeholder). Never resolvable from CI.
^https?://artifactory\.corp(/|:|$)

# Customer-doc code-block URLs that contain a literal PowerShell or shell
# variable expansion the reader is expected to substitute (e.g.
# $quartoVersion, $gitVersion, $(/$pr.number)). The unsubstituted template
# is not a valid fetch target. Generalises the narrower `$UIAO_*` rule
# above, which is retained for documentation value.
\$[A-Za-z(]

# Microsoft Group Policy XML namespace URI — a literal xmlns declaration
# in policy export samples, not a fetchable resource. www.microsoft.com
# returns 404 for this path by design.
^http://www\.microsoft\.com/GroupPolicy/Settings/

# GitHub repository_dispatch endpoint — POST-only URL referenced in CI
# automation runbooks. GET returns 404, which is correct API behavior.
^https://api\.github\.com/repos/[^/]+/[^/]+/dispatches

# Mindcore Techblog (3rd-party blog reference in ADR-002 reference table) —
# host returns connection failure from CI cloud IPs but is a legitimate
# blog post when reachable. Cite stays for human readers; lychee skips.
^https://techblog\.dvbmedia\.net/

# TheTechTrails (3rd-party blog reference in ADR-003) — Google rate-limits
# the redirect chain into a 429 from cloud CI IPs. Same pattern as
# techblog.dvbmedia.net above; legitimate blog when reachable from a
# normal client.
^https?://(www\.)?thetechtrails\.com/

# Local dev-server URLs appear in contributor setup docs (e.g. quickstart,
# contributing) as walkthrough examples, not as live links. Generalises the
# `^http://localhost` exclusion already in the workflow's --exclude list.
^http://127\.0\.0\.1(:[0-9]+)?(/|$)

# piv.idmanagement.gov is referenced in the HRIT modernization proposed-canon
# notes. The federal host serves a TLS certificate valid only for
# coffa.gov / citizenscience.gov / fmi.gov / sftool.gov, so lychee correctly
# rejects it as a TLS mismatch. The misconfiguration is GSA-side; the URL
# stays as a citation for human readers, lychee skips it.
^https://piv\.idmanagement\.gov/

# Defense Counterintelligence and Security Agency (dcsa.mil) — referenced
# in the DCSA Clearance Attribute Service adapter spec and validation
# suite. Federal .mil host returns connection-failed from GitHub Actions
# cloud-IP runners (same geo-gating pattern as learn.microsoft.com and
# fedramp.gov above). The citation is a legitimate human-navigable
# reference; lychee should skip the reachability check.
^https://www\.dcsa\.mil/

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Azure / Microsoft API endpoints in code-block illustrations
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# All of these patterns are API endpoints that appear inside PowerShell or
# REST examples. They return 401/403/404 to unauthenticated cloud CI runners
# (which is correct API behavior — they require a bearer token), but the
# URLs themselves are stable Microsoft hosts. They are documentation, not
# navigable links, so lychee should not assert reachability.

# Azure Resource Manager — `management.azure.com` returns 401 without auth.
^https://management\.azure\.com/

# Microsoft Defender for Endpoint API — 401 without bearer token.
^https://api\.securitycenter\.microsoft\.com/

# Microsoft Fabric Admin REST API — `api.fabric.microsoft.com` returns 404 on
# the root and 401 on /v1 without a bearer token (correct API behavior).
# Referenced in the Book_07a workspace-RBAC reconciliation code block.
^https://api\.fabric\.microsoft\.com/

# Azure DevOps REST API — example URLs include literal {org}/{project}
# placeholders (URL-encoded as %7B/%7D) which 404 by design.
^https://dev\.azure\.com/

# Azure Instance Metadata Service — 169.254.169.254 is link-local and only
# resolvable from inside an Azure VM. Always fails from external CI runners.
^http://169\.254\.169\.254/

# Microsoft Entra OIDC discovery — templated URLs of the form
# https://login.microsoftonline.com/{domain}/v2.0/.well-known/...
# 400 because the {domain} placeholder is unsubstituted.
^https://login\.microsoftonline\.com/

# Power BI service-principal scope — `analysis.windows.net/powerbi/api/.default`
# is the OAuth scope identifier, not a fetchable URL.
^https://analysis\.windows\.net/

# Tenant-specific SharePoint Online hostnames — examples like
# contoso-my.sharepoint.com return TLS-mismatch errors from generic
# resolvers. Tenant SharePoint URLs are illustrative, not navigable.
^https?://[^/]+\.sharepoint\.com/

# Tenant-specific Azure Blob Storage hostnames — fictional storage accounts
# (e.g. orgpathguestconfig.blob.core.windows.net) appear in deployment
# samples and never resolve.
^https?://[^/]+\.blob\.core\.windows\.net/

# Templated raw.githubusercontent URL with literal {org}/{repo} placeholders
# (URL-encoded %7B...%7D) — 400 by design.
^https://raw\.githubusercontent\.com/.*%7B

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Third-party API examples in narrative documentation
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Infoblox Cloud Services Portal — `csp.infoblox.com/api/ddi/v1` is the
# documented base URL for Infoblox DDI; returns 404 to unauthenticated GET
# (which is correct — it requires an authenticated session).
^https://csp\.infoblox\.com/

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fictional internal hostnames used in customer-facing narratives
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# `wiki.internal` is a generic fictional intranet host used in incident
# response runbook examples. Same pattern as the .local and .contoso.*
# rules above.
^https?://wiki\.internal(/|:|$)

# ocsp.agency.gov is a fictional OCSP (Online Certificate Status Protocol)
# placeholder used in PKI/certificate PowerShell examples in the Federal AAN
# Data Protection document (Part 9). The code comment explicitly marks it
# "Replace with actual OCSP URL from CPS". Same class as other fictional
# placeholder hostnames above.
^http://ocsp/$

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# RFC 3161 time-stamping authorities
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# DigiCert's free Authenticode time-stamping endpoint. The host accepts
# Time-Stamp Protocol (TSP) POST requests for code-signing; plain GET
# returns 404 by design. Referenced in the platform-server-build
# code-signing runbook. URL is correct for its purpose; it just isn't
# browseable, which is what lychee tests.
^http://timestamp\.digicert\.com/

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Schema hosting — not yet publicly reachable from CI runners
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# uiao.io hosts the canonical orgpath + SCIM JSON Schemas referenced in
# docs/reference/schemas/*.qmd. The domain is not yet publicly accessible
# from GitHub Actions cloud-IP runners (connection failure). The $id values
# are stable canonical identifiers, not required to resolve at check time.
# Remove this exclusion once uiao.io is publicly reachable.
^https://uiao\.io/schemas/

# GitHub Pages schema mirror (whalermike.github.io/uiao/schemas/) — the
# schema files for image-registry, substrate-manifest, and workspace-contract
# have not yet been published to the Pages site (404). Remove this exclusion
# once the schemas are deployed via the Pages build pipeline.
^https://whalermike\.github\.io/uiao/schemas/


# Placeholder URLs in inbox/ design briefs — illustrative API endpoints,
# not real hosts. Common patterns: *.internal hostnames, company.* /
# company-* example tenants, and the provisioning.microsoft.com/scim/v2/
# SCIM endpoint referenced in HR-driven provisioning briefs.
^https://[^/]+\.internal(/|$)
^https://company[.-]
^https://provisioning\.microsoft\.com/scim/v2/

# contributor-covenant.org intermittently returns "Connection reset by peer"
# (os error 104) from GitHub Actions cloud-IP runners — the reset is host-level
# at their edge, not path-specific (it has hit /translations and /faq in
# different runs, e.g. 27039782952 and 27044847516) and survives lychee's own
# retries. The pages are valid (they resolve from non-CI IPs), so suppress the
# whole host rather than chase individual paths.
^https://www\.contributor-covenant\.org/

# Azure Government SQL data-plane audience shown as an example `-resource` value
# in the Arc / IMDS managed-identity token-validation runbook
# (docs/customer-documents/sql-server-implementation/Book_04.qmd). It is a token
# audience / gov-cloud endpoint base, not a navigable page, and gov-cloud
# endpoints are tenant/geo-gated — lychee gets a connection failure from CI IPs.
^https://database\.usgovcloudapi\.net/

# CISA Binding Operational Directive 25-01 (cyber.dhs.gov) — a real,
# current DHS/CISA directive URL, but cyber.dhs.gov refuses connections
# from GitHub Actions cloud-IP runners (geo/rate-gating), so lychee reports
# "Connection failed". Referenced across ~35 canon/ADR/finding/doc files.
# The host is the false positive, not any single path. Remove once
# cyber.dhs.gov is reliably reachable from CI.
^https://cyber\.dhs\.gov/

# Placeholder ServiceNow instance host used in pipeline-integration docs
# (e.g. docs/customer-documents/platform/ci-pipeline-integration.qmd).
# "yourinstance" is an illustrative tenant, not a real host — mirrors the
# existing your-tenant / company.* placeholder exclusions above.
# Both domains: servicenowservices.com is the Government Community Cloud
# (FedRAMP High / IL-4) host the docs now use; service-now.com is the
# commercial cloud, kept here for any doc that still illustrates it.
^https://yourinstance\.(service-now|servicenowservices)\.com

# Windows Server deployment kit release asset (docs/download/index.qmd) —
# a forward-looking link to the v1.0 release ZIP that is published on the
# first release build (the table row notes "hash generated on first release
# build"), so it 404s until that release is cut. Same "not yet published"
# rationale as the github.io schema-mirror exclusion above. Remove once the
# windows-server-kit-v1.0 release is published.
^https://github\.com/WhalerMike/uiao/releases/download/windows-server-kit-

# Cloud SaaS IaC kit release asset (docs/download/index.qmd) — same
# forward-looking pattern as the windows-server-kit entry above: the iac-kit
# release ZIP is published on the first release build, so it 404s until that
# release is cut. Remove once the iac-kit-v1.0 release is published.
^https://github\.com/WhalerMike/uiao/releases/download/iac-kit-

# OrgPath and OrgMod series kits (docs/download/index.qmd, plus the download
# callouts on the two hub pages) -- same forward-looking pattern as the two
# entries above, with one difference: these assets are not waiting on a cut
# release, they are waiting on the FIRST deploy after the PR that adds them
# merges. The Quarto workflow builds them into _site/download/ and publishes
# them to the rolling downloads-latest release, which only runs on push to
# main -- so the links 404 for exactly as long as the PR is open and are live
# from the first main render onward. Named file-by-file, anchored at both
# ends, so this stays a two-URL bootstrap and never becomes a blanket
# suppression of downloads-latest (the OrgComp/ScubaDrift assets on that same
# release are published and stay checked; tests/test_build_org_family_download.py
# pins that). Remove once a deploy has published both.
^https://github\.com/WhalerMike/uiao/releases/download/downloads-latest/orgpath-governance-series-latest\.zip$
^https://github\.com/WhalerMike/uiao/releases/download/downloads-latest/orgmod-modernization-series-latest\.zip$

# All-DOCX-bundles archive (docs/download/index.qmd) — the "Download all DOCX
# bundles" link points at uiao-all-docx-bundles.zip, which is assembled from the
# per-section *-bundle.docx during the Pages deploy (quarto.yml) and only exists
# in the built _site, never in the source tree. lychee resolves the relative link
# against the repo and reports it missing. Same "deploy-generated, not in source"
# rationale as the section *-bundle.docx tips, which lychee never sees because
# they are injected at deploy time.
uiao-all-docx-bundles\.zip

# Microsoft Tech Community — the techcommunity.microsoft.com host returns
# 502 Bad Gateway or times out from GitHub Actions cloud-IP runners (same
# geo/rate-gating pattern as learn.microsoft.com). Referenced in the
# docs/findings/fedramp-gcc-moderate-informed-network-routing.qmd INR
# announcement URLs. Remove once the host is reliably reachable from CI.
^https://techcommunity\.microsoft\.com/

# MTA-STS placeholder mis-parse — the Infoblox hybrid-DNS whitepaper
# (docs/customer-documents/whitepapers/infoblox-hybrid-dns-unified-ddi.qmd)
# documents the RFC 8461 policy URL as the inline-code template
# `https://mta-sts.<domain>/.well-known/mta-sts.txt`. lychee strips the
# `<domain>` angle-bracket placeholder and extracts a bare `https://mta-sts/`,
# which has no TLD and fails with "Connection failed". The documented template
# is correct for human readers; the host is a parser artifact, not a real link.
# Same class as the `{domain}` / `$UIAO_*` placeholder suppressions above.
^https://mta-sts/$

# OMB M-21-31 PDF (whitehouse.gov) — the Biden-era OMB memorandum
# "Improving the Federal Government's Investigative and Remediation
# Capabilities Related to Cybersecurity Incidents" (August 2021) was
# removed from whitehouse.gov during the 2025 administration transition.
# The specific PDF path `/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/M-21-31-*.pdf`
# returns 404. This is a historical federal mandate reference (cited in
# the FedRAMP GCC-Moderate boundary-impact spec); the document remains
# authoritative but the whitehouse.gov copy is no longer hosted.
# Re-examine once a stable alternative host (e.g. NARA, OMB.gov) is
# confirmed accessible from CI.
^https://www\.whitehouse\.gov/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/M-21-31-

# ScubaDrift standalone download set (docs/download/index.qmd) — scubadrift-latest.zip,
# the versioned scubadrift-<version>.zip, and ScubaDrift-Documentation.docx are
# generated from tools/scubadrift/ at deploy time by
# scripts/build_scubadrift_download.py (quarto.yml assemble job) and only exist in
# the published _site/download/, not in source. Same "deploy-generated, not in
# source" class as uiao-all-docx-bundles.zip above, so a source-relative check
# reports them missing.
scubadrift-latest\.zip
scubadrift-[0-9][^/]*\.zip
ScubaDrift-Documentation\.docx

# Absolute published-site links that 404 while the site is in MAINTENANCE MODE
# (quarto.yml env.MAINTENANCE_MODE="true"): only the root placeholder and
# /download/ are served, so links to https://whalermike.github.io/uiao/<section>/
# return 404. These resolve normally once the full site is restored — REMOVE
# these two entries when MAINTENANCE_MODE is set back to "false".
#   docs/customer-documents/index.qmd
^https://whalermike\.github\.io/uiao/customer-documents/$
#   docs/customer-documents/validation-suites/AUTHORING.md
^https://whalermike\.github\.io/uiao/customer-documents/validation-suites/$

# ServiceNow Developer Portal — developer.servicenow.com times out from
# GitHub Actions cloud-IP runners (connection timeout, not a broken link).
# Referenced in docs/academy/contributor-tier-1-setup.qmd. The host is
# a legitimate public resource; CI geo/rate-gating is the cause.
^https://developer\.servicenow\.com/

# ServiceNow press-room release — www.servicenow.com rejects lychee's HTTP/2
# with a protocol error (and 403s other automated fetchers); a live page behind
# bot protection, not a broken link. Referenced in the Vol 0 Book 01 and Vol III
# Book 07 product-inventory / evidence-fabric tables.
^https://www\.servicenow\.com/company/media/press-room/servicenow-secures-fedramp-high-authorization\.html$

# ServiceNow product page — same www.servicenow.com HTTP/2 bot-wall as the
# press-room entry above, on a different path. Referenced in the
# ticket-to-machine-not-ticket-to-human whitepaper's certificate-lifecycle
# References table.
^https://www\.servicenow\.com/products/certificate-management\.html$

# ServiceNow IntegrationHub subscription addendum (PDF on ServiceNow's own
# asset CDN) — same www.servicenow.com HTTP/2 bot-wall as the two entries
# above, on a /content/dam/ path; lychee reports "HTTP/2 protocol error",
# not a 404. Referenced in the ticket-to-machine-standalone whitepaper's
# section 13, where it is cited as the document that governs which
# IntegrationHub spokes a subscription actually entitles. Not fetched from
# the authoring environment (egress to servicenow.com is blocked there), so
# the suppression rests on the failure mode matching the entries above.
^https://www\.servicenow\.com/content/dam/servicenow-assets/public/en-us/doc-type/legal/snc-addendum-integrationhub\.pdf$

# Juniper Access Assurance product page — same HTTP/2-protocol-error bot wall
# as the www.servicenow.com entries above (lychee's HTTP/2 handshake is
# rejected while the page is live for real clients). Failed identically on
# PR #1410's Link Check across two separate runs ~9 minutes apart, each after
# lychee's own 3 internal retries — persistent CI-side false positive, not
# link rot. Referenced in Vol I Book 07 (Network Enforcement Substrate).
^https://www\.juniper\.net/us/en/products/cloud-services/access-assurance\.html$

# Broadcom VCF TechDocs — techdocs.broadcom.com returns 404 to automated
# fetchers on this deep link while the page is live (top search result, matching
# content). Bot protection, not link rot. Referenced in Vol I Book 01 and the
# SSA Landing Zone note.
^https://techdocs\.broadcom\.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vcf-9-0-and-later/9-1/advanced-network-management/ip-address-management-ipam/integrate-nsx-with-infoblox\.html$

# Infoblox NIOS 9.0 certificate-management page — docs.infoblox.com is
# intermittently unreachable from GitHub Actions cloud-IP runners (run
# 29152119495 failed with "Network unreachable" against this exact deep link
# while a full main-branch link-check passed hours earlier). The page remains a
# legitimate human-readable reference in the SSA Landing Zone IPAM/DDI/PKI docs;
# suppress the CI-only reachability check for this specific URL.
^https://docs\.infoblox\.com/space/nios90/280266962$

# Fictional SSA SBOM/VEX endpoint used as an illustrative example URL in
# Book_12 (Supply Chain Risk Management). The hostname sbom.agency.gov does not
# currently exist — the URL shows what an agency-hosted VEX endpoint would
# look like once BOD 26-04 obligations are fulfilled. Same class as
# ocsp.agency.gov and other placeholder agency hostnames above.
^https://sbom/$

# OpenTofu homepage returns 502 Bad Gateway from GitHub Actions cloud-IP
# runners (server-side error, not a broken link). Referenced in
# docs/customer-documents/adapter-specs/terraform/terraform.qmd and its
# validation-suite counterpart. The site is a real public resource;
# CI geo/rate-gating is the cause. Same suppression pattern as other
# hosts that behave correctly for real clients but 5xx from CI cloud IPs.
^https://opentofu\.org/

# Exact public docs that failed in main-branch Link Check run 29428050280 with
# "Connection reset by peer" from GitHub Actions runners. They are legitimate
# vendor/specification pages referenced in canon/docs, so suppress the
# CI-specific reachability failure rather than rewriting valid citations.
^https://developers\.login\.gov/$
^https://docs\.snowflake\.com/en/user-guide/security-mfa-rollout$
^https://docs\.aws\.amazon\.com/inspector/latest/user/what-is-inspector\.html$
^https://peps\.python\.org/pep-0420/$

# eGroup (egroup-us.com) intermittently refuses connections from GitHub
# Actions cloud-IP runners — the same URLs resolved with 200 (via redirect)
# in run 28727857911 hours before failing with "Connection failed" in run
# 28739160519. Referenced in inbox/Integrate the Intune-Entra-Arc Track/.
# Real public site; CI-side connection flakiness is the cause.
^https://(www\.)?egroup-us\.com/

# Fictional NDES/SCEP enrollment endpoint (ndes.tenant.onmicrosoft.com) used
# as an illustrative Intune SCEP connector URL in the Book_02 NAC certificate
# enrollment example. The onmicrosoft.com tenant is a placeholder; the URL
# shows the shape of an NDES mscep.dll endpoint and is never resolvable from
# CI. Same class as ocsp.agency.gov and sbom.agency.gov above.
^https://ndes/$

# Templated download URLs with unsubstituted ${VAR} brace expansions, e.g.
# https://releases.hashicorp.com/terraform/${TF_VERSION}/terraform_${TF_VERSION}_linux_amd64.zip
# in the AWS CodePipeline buildspec example (infoblox-ddi-book/aws-lz-automation/
# pipelines/codepipeline-aws-ddi.md). The reader substitutes the version; the raw
# ${...} literal is not a fetch target. Braces form is not caught by the
# \$[A-Za-z(] rule above. Same class as the $UIAO_* / $[A-Za-z(] placeholder rules.
\$(\{|%7[Bb])

# ServiceNow Store and product-docs pages gate automated fetchers (same class as
# developer.servicenow.com already ignored above): store.servicenow.com returns a
# JS/bot-wall and www.servicenow.com/docs 403s cloud-CI IPs. Referenced in the
# Infoblox DDI ServiceNow-orchestration chapter/packages as product citations.
^https://store\.servicenow\.com/
^https://www\.servicenow\.com/docs/

# DISA APL (Approved Products List) portal times out for cloud-CI runners —
# same class as the ServiceNow bot-walls above. Cited in the AAN network-
# enforcement material as the DoDIN APL authority; the site is reachable
# interactively but not from GitHub-hosted runners, and it flaked Link Check
# on main at merge dd401b8e (2026-07-14).
^https://aplits\.disa\.mil/

# Hostnames parameterised for the SSA/federal editions (#1239). lychee parses
# `https://sbom.{{< meta agency.domain >}}/...` only up to the `{`, so the URL it
# extracts is the bare host below. A URL carrying a variable is a template, not a
# link -- unresolvable by construction, in either edition.

# Federal AAN download kit (docs/download/index.qmd + the series index.qmd) —
# orgcomp-federal-series-latest.zip and orgcomp-series-bundle.docx are built into
# _site/download/ and _site/customer-documents/orgcomp-series/ on every Pages
# deploy (quarto.yml: build_orgcomp_download.py + bundle_section_docx.py). They never
# exist in the source tree, so lychee resolves the relative links against the repo
# and reports them missing. Same "deploy-generated, not in source" rationale as the
# ScubaDrift zip and uiao-all-docx-bundles.zip above.
orgcomp-federal-series-latest\.zip
orgcomp-series-bundle\.docx
# NOTE: the per-volume Word bundles used to be suppressed here. That rule was
# added when the links were repo-relative (deploy-generated, not in source), and
# it silently outlived its reason: once the links became absolute release URLs
# it masked 11 genuinely broken ones, because GitHub rewrites spaces/commas/
# ampersands in asset names to '.' and the docs linked the percent-encoded
# original. Filenames are URL-safe at generation now
# (bundle_section_docx.py::_url_safe), so these links are checked like any other.
# Do not re-add a suppression here without re-reading that history.

# RFC Editor (www.rfc-editor.org) — went unhealthy for GitHub Actions cloud-IP
# runners mid-day 2026-07-19: /rfc/rfc6749 returned 502 Bad Gateway, /rfc/rfc4510
# timed out, and the /rfc/rfcNNNN URLs redirect-looped 302 -> /info/rfcNNNN/ four
# deep (Link Check run 29684133061, attempts 1 and 2 — the same links passed at
# 10:48 UTC the same day, so this is host-side instability, not link rot). The
# RFC citations (Book_25_CPT_08 and others) are canonical, stable identifiers;
# suppress the CI reachability check rather than rewrite valid citations.
# Re-examine once rfc-editor.org is reliably reachable from CI.
^https://www\.rfc-editor\.org/

# Sinnathurai blog (3rd-party blog reference in ADR-002, alongside the
# techblog.dvbmedia.net entry above) — a Medium-hosted custom domain whose
# redirect chain bounces through medium.com and intermittently 504s /
# 307-loops from GitHub Actions cloud-IP runners (504 Gateway Timeout in
# Link Check run 29684906627 after redirect-looping in earlier runs the
# same day). Legitimate blog post when reachable from a normal client;
# cite stays for human readers, lychee skips the reachability check.
^https://blog\.sinnathurai\.ch/

# FedRAMP OSCAL namespace URI — `https://fedramp.gov/ns/oscal` is the `ns`
# value on OSCAL props (e.g. in the ongoing-authorization evidence generator
# code excerpted in B.1.4 §6.4, and in fedramp-3pao-evidence-interface.md /
# fedramp-20x-integration.md). It is a namespace identifier, not a fetchable
# page: it redirects fedramp.gov -> www.fedramp.gov -> 404. Same class as the
# Microsoft Group Policy XML namespace URI exclusion above.
^https://fedramp\.gov/ns/oscal

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local targets that do not exist yet (surfaced when `^file://` was
# narrowed to `^file://.*\.html$` on 2026-08-25)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The blanket `^file://` rule had been hiding every broken relative link in
# the repo. Narrowing it surfaced 260; 217 were stale paths from the ADR-032
# module consolidation and the ADR-083 docs reorganization and are fixed. The
# rest — below — are links to content that was planned and never produced, so
# there is no path to correct. Each entry names what has to be authored before
# it can be removed. They are listed by exact target so a NEW broken link in
# the same directory still fails the gate.

# Word-conversion figures for the SSA Landing Zone IPAM/FedRAMP paper. The
# .docx embedded eleven images; the conversion kept the references and dropped
# the media/ directory. Remove once the figures are extracted or redrawn into
# docs/customer-documents/orgcomp-series/media/.
docs/customer-documents/orgcomp-series/media/image[0-9]+\.png$

# Provisional patent application figures (inbox/drafts). The application text
# calls out nine figures that have not been drawn. Remove once
# inbox/drafts/patent-figures/ is populated.
inbox/drafts/patent-figures/figure-[0-9]+\.png$

# OrgPath-on-Azure service diagrams cited by two inbox drafts. The drafts were
# written against a diagram set that was never rendered into inbox/images/.
inbox/images/orgpath-azure-service-diagram-[0-9]+-[a-z-]+\.(png|svg)$

# Vol 07a "UIAO Beneath the Azure SSOT Stack" — cited by the SQL Server adapter
# interface and one inbox draft. Only the chapter's images/ exist; the .qmd
# itself was never written. Remove once the chapter lands.
docs/customer-documents/orgpath-narrative/07a-uiao-beneath-the-azure-ssot-stack\.qmd$

# drift-engine-config.yaml — UIAO_009 and Spec2-D3.1 cite a canonical drift
# engine config under canon/data/orgpath/. Only its JSON Schema exists
# (src/uiao/schemas/orgpath/drift-engine-config.schema.json); the instance
# document has not been authored.
src/uiao/canon/data/orgpath/drift-engine-config\.yaml$

# Single unwritten targets, each cited once:
#   UIAO_205 §"Sponsor onboarding" cites an SSP spec that does not exist
src/uiao/canon/specs/system-security-plan\.md$
#   UIAO_207 cites a FedRAMP OSCAL package spec that does not exist
src/uiao/canon/specs/fedramp-oscal-package-spec\.md$
#   the AGD SailPoint connector guide cites a platform page that was never added
docs/platform/active-governance-directory\.qmd$
#   inbox/drafts/SUMMARY.md cites an onboarding guide that was never written
docs/onboarding/adapter-developer-guide\.md$
